src/test/stress/DESIGN

Plan: a pluggable stress suite
==============================

This is the design for reworking src/test/stress from 36 hand-written
files into a composable suite.  Nothing here is upstream yet, so this is
a rewrite rather than a migration: names, numbering and the contents of
StressConcurrently.pm are all free to change.

Why rework it at all
====================

The suite has two jobs and currently does neither cleanly.

- Discovery.  Run randomized concurrent workloads against the
  CONCURRENTLY commands and see what breaks.  This is what the suite is
  good at: it has found five distinct backend bugs so far.

- Regression.  Once a bug is fixed, keep it fixed.  The suite is weak
  here, and the weakness is structural: 016_foreign_keys.pl reproduced
  the RI conindid race about once in 15 runs at the default duration, so
  a CI run would have passed roughly 93% of the time with the bug
  reintroduced.  A probabilistic test is a bug finder, not a guard.

Meanwhile the files themselves duplicate heavily: 24 of 36 declare the
same tbl(id int PRIMARY KEY, val int), and 13 share the same DDL
rotation verbatim.  Adding a new dimension means copying a file and
editing the interesting 20%, which is why gaps (row locks, subxacts,
index-only scans, exclusion constraints) went unnoticed until someone
went looking.

The plan therefore splits the two jobs, and makes the discovery half
composable so that coverage is enumerable instead of archaeological.

Layer 0: three changes worth making regardless
==============================================

These are independent of the restructuring and each is small.

1. Diagnosability by default.  stress_init_node() sets

       log_error_verbosity = verbose
       backtrace_functions = 'relation_open'
       log_lock_waits = on

   and, for standby environments, log_recovery_conflict_waits = on.
   None of these produce output unless something goes wrong.  Two of
   the bugs diagnosed so far took ~30 re-runs to pin down purely
   because the failing message arrived without a call site; with a
   backtrace it was one run.  The same applies to the standby standoff,
   which only became readable once recovery conflict waits were logged.

2. Seeded randomness.  stress_seed() takes stress_seed=N from
   PG_TEST_EXTRA (or picks and reports one), seeds Perl's rand, and is
   passed to pgbench as --random-seed.  Several tests choose variants
   with a bare int(rand(2)), so today two runs of the same file test
   different things and a failure cannot be replayed.

3. One REPACK tolerance helper.  REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) is not MVCC-safe
   yet: a snapshot spanning its relfilenode swap may see a table empty.
   Roughly ten files open-code the "tolerate empty, hold anything else
   to the full invariant" dance.  Behind one helper it can be disabled
   in one place (stress_strict_mvcc=1) to check whether REPACK has
   become MVCC-safe, and deleted in one place when it has.

Layer 1: pgbench's schema as the base
=====================================

Use pgbench -i -s N rather than bespoke per-file setup, at two scales:
s=1, where the single branch row makes every tpcb-like transaction
collide, and s=50, where the indexes are large enough to be multi-level
and a concurrent build has real work to do.  Both are in the CI set.
This buys:

- Scalable loading with no setup code per scenario.

- A stronger invariant than the current single-column sum.  Every
  tpcb-like transaction applies the same delta to one account, one
  teller and one branch, and records it, so at every commit boundary

      sum(abalance) = sum(tbalance) = sum(bbalance) = sum(delta)

  across pgbench_accounts, pgbench_tellers, pgbench_branches and
  pgbench_history.  That is a four-way cross-check over four relations
  instead of one column of one table, and it comes with a realistic hot
  spot: with a small scale factor the branch rows are heavily
  contended.

- A natural spread of DDL targets in one schema: a large table
  (accounts), a tiny one (branches), an append-only one (history).

Two adjustments are needed.  pgbench_history has no primary key, so it
gets one before REPACK can decode it; and the scale factor becomes a
knob, since it trades table size against contention.

Bespoke schemas do not disappear entirely.  What fits the model gets
ported: the TOAST payload/md5 pair and the exclusion-constraint slots
become schema decorators plus their own check plugins, since both are
really "an extra column shape and an extra invariant" on top of an
ordinary workload.  What does not fit stays hand-written: the SSI
gapless sequence, whose whole point is a table with no unique
constraint and a very specific transaction shape, and pg_dump's
dump-and-restore comparison, which is an external process rather than a
workload.  Those two keep their own files and pick up only the Layer 0
helpers.

Layer 2: five registries
========================

Everything a scenario is made of lives in a named registry entry.  Each
entry declares what it needs and what it cannot be combined with, so a
bad combination fails when the scenario is built, with a clear message,
rather than halfway through a run.

  schema   pgbench, +toast_column, +generated, +identity, +partitioned,
           +exclusion_slot, +fk_child, +matview, +jsonb_column

  indexes  btree_bid, btree_abalance, unique_expr, covering, partial,
           expr_txid, gin_jsonb, gist_range, brin_aid, hash_bid, spgist

  load     tpcb_like, simple_update, select_only, bulk_copy,
           upsert_merge, cursor_hold, plancache, subxact_churn,
           row_lock, twophase, toast_rewrite, generated_update,
           replica_role_apply

  ddl      index:       create_index_concurrently, drop_index_concurrently,
                        reindex_index_concurrently, reindex_table_concurrently,
                        reindex_blocking
           repack:      repack_concurrently, repack_using_index, repack_blocking
           partition:   attach_partition, detach_partition_concurrently,
                        detach_finalize, create_partition, drop_partition,
                        split_partition, merge_partition,
                        partitionwise_index_build
           table:       alter_table_rewrite, set_unlogged, truncate
           maintenance: vacuum, vacuum_freeze, analyze
           matview:     refresh_matview_concurrently

  check    balances, row_counts, amcheck, index_vs_seq, ios_vs_seq,
           visibility_map, row_lock_durability, no_slot_leak,
           decoding_off, no_orphans, distinct_slots, replica_match,
           dump_restore

  env      standalone, standby, subscription, cross_database, crash_loop,
           cancellation, lock_exhaustion

How many of those DDL commands are in flight at once is itself a
dimension, not a property of the harness:

  ddl_concurrency => 1      one advisory-lock gate, one command at a time
                   => N      a gate per table, N commands overlapping
                   => none   no gate at all

Overlapping REPACKs are what exposed the logical-decoding activation
race, so this belongs in the matrix rather than in one special file.

The partition family deserves its own note: attaching and detaching
partitions, splitting and merging them, and the documented
build-per-partition-then-attach dance for a partitioned index all change
the shape of a relation while CONCURRENTLY commands are mid-flight, and
each of them interacts differently with the partition descriptor a
concurrent query is holding.  It is gated by +partitioned.

Environments carry their own GUCs, because getting those wrong is itself
a source of false failures: the standby environment sets a finite
max_standby_streaming_delay (never -1, which lets replay wait forever on
a reader that is itself blocked on a lock replay holds) and runs pgbench
with --max-tries so that recovery-conflict cancellations are retried
rather than aborting the run; lock_exhaustion sets a deliberately small
max_locks_per_transaction; and so on.

Composition uses pgbench's own mechanism
========================================

pgbench already supports weighted script composition:

    pgbench -f load.sql@3 -f ddl.sql@1 -f checks.sql@2

The current suite welds everything into a single script and then
generates \if chains to pick a branch -- which is what
stress_variant_switch() and the switch inside stress_ddl_gate() are for.
With one script per plugin those disappear.  The advisory-lock gate
survives, as its own script, because serializing the DDL client is still
wanted.

Layer 3: scenarios, CI set and soak set
=======================================

A scenario is a named tuple:

    repack_dml => {
        schema  => ['pgbench'],
        indexes => ['btree_abalance'],
        load    => ['tpcb_like@3', 'row_lock@1'],
        ddl     => ['repack_concurrently', 'reindex_table_concurrently',
                    'drop_create_index'],
        checks  => ['balances', 'amcheck', 'index_vs_seq'],
        env     => 'standalone',
        tags    => ['ci'],
    }

Each scenario gets a small stub file under t/ so that meson and prove
see stable test names, run them in parallel and keep per-test logs; the
stub is a few lines and the substance lives in the catalogue.

  CI set     A fixed list of about ten scenarios chosen for breadth per
             second, roughly six seconds each.  Fixed, not sampled: a CI
             failure has to be reproducible and attributable to a name.

  Soak set   stress_mode=soak in PG_TEST_EXTRA, on the same suite, walks
             the whole catalogue plus seeded random combinations under a
             wall-clock budget at a high stress_concurrently.  This is
             where the discovery value actually is; the six-second
             default rarely catches anything.

A --list mode prints the dimension-by-scenario coverage matrix, so gaps
are mechanical to spot rather than something someone has to go looking
for.

Deterministic regression guards: deferred
=========================================

Injection-point tests that would fail every time rather than one run in
fifteen are out of scope for now.  The consequence is worth stating
plainly so it is a choice rather than an oversight: the fixed races are
guarded probabilistically, and at the default duration a reintroduced
bug would usually go unnoticed by a single CI run.  Soak mode is what
covers them, and it only covers them if it actually runs.

One race is not covered at all by any amount of soaking: the
SxactGlobalXmin window in SetNewSxactGlobalXmin(), which is too narrow
to hit without an injection point.

Reproducing the standby standoff
================================

One target for the new system, once it exists: the replay-versus-reader
standoff found while fixing the planner's handling of a concurrently
dropped index.  Replay acquires an AccessExclusiveLock and then waits on
a snapshot conflict with a reader that is itself blocked on that lock;
hot standby's deadlock check does not cover that shape, so with
max_standby_streaming_delay = -1 nothing breaks the cycle.

That needs a standby environment with a -1 delay, readers holding a
snapshot across a statement boundary, and a DDL rotation that makes
replay take relation locks -- all of which are ordinary registry
entries.  If a scenario built from them reproduces it reliably, that is
the reproducer to take to -hackers.

Constraints the implementation must hold to
===========================================

- Every generated script is written into the test's basedir and the
  exact pgbench command line is logged.  A declarative layer that cannot
  be reproduced by hand outside the harness is worse than the
  duplication it replaces.

- A bespoke escape hatch stays supported.  Not every invariant fits the
  model, and the ones that do not are among the most valuable tests.

- The CI set stays a fixed list.  Combination explosion belongs in soak
  mode, behind a seed.

How it lands
============

In one pass: the registries, the catalogue, the stub files and the
deletion of the 36 existing files in a single commit.  The suite is not
upstream, so there is no interim state to keep working and no history to
preserve.  The risk that buys is a large commit, and the mitigation is
that the old files stay in the branch's history to compare against --
every invariant they check must appear as a check plugin or a bespoke
file, and that mapping is worth writing down as the port proceeds.
